The one pot one step combination of the radical and the ring-opening routes in water (represented in Scheme 3
                           ) is presented entry 3 and the resulting 1H NMR spectrum is shown Fig. 1
                           . The assignments were made from the 1H NMR spectra of starch and of poly(butyl acrylate) that are given in the Appendix B in Supplementary material.

The resulting grafting percentage is of the same order of magnitude than the one obtained using only butyl acrylate in similar conditions (e.gentries 5-6-7 vs. 1-3-4). The determination of the individual grafting ratio, i.e. poly(butyl acrylate-co-styrene) vs. oligo(ε-caprolactone) is not possible by the 1H NMR method used previously due to signal overlapping, and we did not find any other suitable method despite several trials. However, the ratio of butyl acrylate vs. styrene units in the grafted chains could be determined by 1H NMR analysis as reported in the Appendix G in Supplementary material. It can be seen that the composition of the poly(butyl acrylate-co-styrene) grafts follows the monomer feed. The molecular weights follow the same trends as those observed while grafting PBA, i.e. M
                           n values lower than those obtained from the radical process alone, the difference between the two processes being less important in the presence of styrene. This could be explained by radical chains scissions. It is indeed known that acrylates favor the occurrence of termination reaction by disproportionation while termination by combination is the main process with styrene as the monomer (see Scheme 4
                           ) (Nakamura & Yamago, 2015). This may also explain that the dispersities observed in the presence of the three monomers are lower than those observed in the absence of styrene (2.5–4.4 vs. 3.8–7.7).
